Original commit body from 32e7c32d894be95fc6cb96e697a7c298fb7917d6:

WI-fasub. `plain = decrypt(t); safe = encrypt(plain); write(safe)` reported an
UNSANITIZED flow about code that visibly sanitizes. The BFS barrier exempts the
seed -- it must, the seed is the taint origin and has to stay reachable -- and
that same exemption means a barrier called FROM the seed function is never
consulted. Encrypt-then-write in one function is an ordinary shape, so the false
violation was systematic.

THE BARRIER HELPER CANNOT FIX IT, AND THAT IS STRUCTURAL. _reachability_past_
sanitizers sees a call GRAPH: "handler calls encrypt" and "handler calls write"
are two edges with no order between them, and the graph is byte-identical
whichever order the source has. No refinement of call-graph reachability can
distinguish encrypt-then-write from write-then-encrypt. Answering it needs
statement ordering inside the seed function -- stmt_defuse, PR #203 -- which
reaches propagate_taint_ddg alone. So the fix lives at that caller, and
propagate_taint_structural can NEVER honour this shape, for any language. That
is a permanent scope limit, not a deferral, and it covers most of the catalogue
because every language without a def/use extractor is served by that pass.

TWO WALKS, BOTH HALVES LOAD-BEARING. Marking a flow sanitized suppresses it from
a claim's violation set, so it needs positive evidence exactly as section 3a's
removal arm does. _ddg_taint_reaches gained `barrier_lines` and now runs twice:
unrestricted (is there a data route source->sink at all?) and with the sanitizer
call sites as barriers (does any route AVOID the barrier?). The label is earned
only when the first says True and the second says False -- exhausted with every
step accounted for. The barrier walk's None never earns it: "I lost track of it"
is not "you protected it" (L58, applied to sanitization rather than removal).

MUTATION-TESTED, because six of the ten tests passed BEFORE the fix and would
otherwise be vacuous (L17). Four guards, each reddening exactly its own test and
nothing else: accepting None as protection; dropping the confirmed-dependence
precondition; making the barrier flag an escape instead of accounting for it;
and claiming protection whenever any barrier exists in the function.

ONE HOME FOR THE SANITIZER LINES. Collected by the shared registrar, not
re-derived at the point of use, so the INV-finoh resolution-/kind-aware gate
governs both. Re-matching sanitizers at a second site is how this module once
acquired a private, ungated copy of that registration which silently deleted
real flows. _edge_call_sites likewise folds the meta.call_lines parse into one
home now that two consumers need it.

KNOWN RESIDUAL, STATED RATHER THAN LEFT AS FOLKLORE. The barrier drops every
heir at its line, which is wrong for a statement that BOTH sanitizes and rebinds
the raw value (`safe, leak = encrypt(plain), plain`). The conservative
alternative -- treat >1 heir as ambiguous -- was rejected on measurement, not
taste: Go's `ct, err := aead.Seal(...)` binds two names from EVERY sanitizer call
in that language, so it would forfeit the fix wherever a multiple-return language
is involved, trading a rare false negative for a systematic false positive. The
two cases are indistinguishable from the DDG alone (one statement row,
defines=(a,b) uses=(x,) in both). A sanitizer and sink on the SAME line
(`write(encrypt(plain))`) likewise still reports unsanitized.

COHORT EFFECT: ZERO, BY CONSTRUCTION, AND ESTABLISHED WITHOUT RUNNING AN A/B
(L59). The fix fires only where a sanitizer is REGISTERED against a function, so
that gate was measured first with production's own registrar: 0 sanitizer call
sites across 95,950 taint call edges in all nine repos, with a synthetic
Fernet.encrypt edge confirming the instrument fires (L9). Decomposed one level
further, because a zero has two readings with identical evidence: exactly ONE
pre-gate name hit exists cohort-wide (pretix, a bare `encrypt`), and it is
refused correctly -- call_construct: method on an unresolved edge is precisely
what INV-finoh's gate exists to reject, since binding a bare x.encrypt() to
Fernet.encrypt would falsely sanitize a real flow. The cohort's zero is ABSENCE,
not refusal. Report it as prevention, not an improvement; its value scales with
the sanitizer catalogue rather than with today's output.

PUBLISHED SCOPE FOR INV-karud CLAUSE (b). The clause holds where it can be
measured, and that population is far smaller than the source/sink catalogue's:
12 entries, 5 languages, every one cryptographic (plaintext -> ciphertext,
key_material -> derived_key). So a repository-wide "0 sanitized flows" is
ambiguous in exactly the L58 way -- nothing was protected, or nothing COULD be,
because the claims' taint labels and the sanitizers' input labels are disjoint
sets. New `sanitizer_scope` block nested in `dataflow_coverage` (envelope schema
1.7 -> 1.8, additive, on BOTH the JSON and text surfaces): catalogue size,
languages carrying entries, the input->output categories it can express,
`sanitizable_labels`, and `same_function_honoured_by`. That last is a declared
constant with a test on it (R16) -- it reads ["ddg"], and the test fails the
moment the structural pass gains the ability the constant says it lacks.
Categories and labels are DERIVED from the loaded catalogue, so extending it
moves the disclosure automatically instead of requiring an edit by whoever is
least likely to re-read it.

ADR-0017 amended in place at section 3b (the structural pass's permanent limit)
and section 3e (sanitizer_scope); spec's stale VERIFY_CLAIMS_SCHEMA_VERSION
corrected 1.3 -> 1.8 and its structural-pass bullet now carries the limit.

